I meant that the fact that a missed dagger doesn't anger a dwarf
two-thirds of the time made sense, in that it seems to me that a
peaceful intelligent monster would be likely to give the benefit of the
doubt in most cases- i.e. would assume that a dagger flying by _wasn't_
meant as an attack, except in cases where said dagger came particularly
close. If 2/3 of daggers go wider of the mark than to be in that "this
must be aggression" zone, then it makes sense that
thrown-but-poorly-aimed daggers would only, on average, anger their
would-be targets one time in three.



